Today, I downloaded the "official" IBM-PC binaries to NetHack 3.0.7 from
linc.cis.upenn.edu, and while I was installing them, I noticed two things.
First, the install.bat file uses a method to check for the existence of a
directory which then seems to preclude the creation of that directory; a
minor problem, fixed by having the nethack directory already created.

More importantly, the files are all compressed into self-extracters created
with LHarc 1.14 - the version which was hacked by an unknown hacker to be
named "LHice" and use .ICE instead of .LZH!  It's never been determined
what other modifications may have been made to the code, but the author of
LHarc has verified that he never intended to change the extension or program
in this fashion, and most copies of LH114 were wiped out immediately.  There
is a valid version called LH114B (available from chyde.uwasa.fi), but LHice
should *not* be used!

LHarc does provide the best compression on IBM systems, at the expense of
speed.  But I think the PC binaries should be repacked with LH113 or a
different utility - LHice could be dangerous.  Anyone know what ICE stands
for in the cyberpunk/computing world?  It's not a pleasant thing.

